title: a remote that is empty for the wrong reason reconciles the whole board away
updated: 2026-08-10T01:27:58Z
---

Left open deliberately by the tracking-mirror fix (commit bea4a8d), recorded so
the trade-off is visible rather than forgotten.

With the mirror in place, "the remote had these refs at the last sync and does
not have them now" is a well-founded observation, and acting on it is what makes
a `karr delete` propagate and a `karr destroy` take effect across clones. But it
is indistinguishable from a remote that is empty for the wrong reason:

  * origin re-created or re-initialised
  * the remote URL edited to point somewhere else
  * a hosting-side restore that rolled the namespace back

In each of those a routine writing command reconciles the local board down to
nothing, in one step, silently — it looks exactly like the legitimate case.

Not a regression: before the mirror, pull could not delete anything at all,
because it never applied a remote change in the first place (the bug that fix
addressed). So this is a new failure mode introduced together with a much larger
one being removed. Bounded, but worth a net.

Options, roughly in order of how much they cost:

  * refuse a wholesale wipe — if reconciliation would delete *every* task ref,
    stop and require `--yes` or an explicit `karr sync --prune`
  * scale it: refuse when more than N% of the board would disappear at once
  * park deletions the way case 4 parks overwritten refs
    (`refs/karr-conflict/<remote>/<name>`), so a mistaken wipe is recoverable

The first is probably enough and is a few lines in the reconciliation loop.
Whatever is chosen needs a test for the legitimate path too: `karr destroy` on
one clone must still propagate to another.
Fixed and merged into main; full suite green at 88 files / 566 tests.

Every one of these was reproduced before it was fixed and left a regression test
that was verified to fail against the pre-fix code -- not a test that merely
passes now. Details and what was deliberately NOT fixed are in the merge commit
that names this ticket.
